#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse a commit that does not pass scripts/test.sh.
#
# ⛔ WHY THIS EXISTS, because it is slow and someone will want to remove it.
#
# In one session, four commits in this workspace asserted a property that
# was false at the moment they were made: two claimed "clippy clean" with
# warnings outstanding, one claimed a commit message that the shell had
# silently eaten, and one claimed "all gates pass" while scripts/test.sh
# exited nonzero. Every one had the same mechanism: the check and the
# commit happened in the same breath, so the check's result arrived too
# late to change anything.
#
# THE FOURTH HAPPENED WHILE COMMITTING THE SCRIPT WRITTEN TO PREVENT IT.
# A mechanism that defeats the person who has just built the countermeasure
# is structural, not a lapse of attention that more attention fixes.
#
# So the ordering is enforced by git rather than remembered: a nonzero exit
# refuses the commit. The check now precedes the action because it must.
#
# ⚠ DO NOT MAKE THIS SKIPPABLE FOR CONVENIENCE. No blanket --no-verify
# habit, no "WIP commits are exempt". For a crypto crate a slow commit is
# the correct cost, and the exemption is precisely where the fifth instance
# would live. If it becomes too slow to bear, MAKE THE SCRIPT FASTER.
#
# ⚠ The honest escape hatch, because one will genuinely be needed: commit
# with `--no-verify` AND say so in the commit message, with the reason. An
# undocumented bypass that everyone uses silently is worse than a
# documented one used twice.
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
echo "pre-commit: scripts/test.sh"
exec ./scripts/test.sh
